<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch9-section-isobaric-coordinates-subsection-omega" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Interpretation of <m>\omega</m></title>

    <p>
        Under hydrostatic conditions,
        as an air parcel rises,
        its pressure decreases as it encounters lower pressure environments,
        and it expands to match the lower environmental pressure.
        The reverse is true as an air parcel sinks.
        Therefore,
        the pressure vertical velocity <m>\omega \lt 0</m> for rising motion while the vertical velocity <m>w \gt 0</m>,
        and <m>\omega \gt 0</m> for sinking motion while <m>w \lt 0</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        We can make this opposing relationship between <m>\omega</m> and <m>w</m> concrete by using total differentiation to expand <m>\omega</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-omega-total-derivative" number="yes">
            \omega
            =
            \frac{Dp}{Dt}
            =
            \frac{\partial p}{\partial t}
            + u \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
            + v \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
            + w \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Applying hydrostatic balance,
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \omega
                \amp =
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial t}
                + u \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
                + v \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
                + w (-\rho g)
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-omega-w-relationship-full" number="yes">
                \amp =
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial t}
                + u \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
                + v \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
                - w \rho g
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="holton-hakim" text="custom">Holton and Hakim (2013)</xref>
        show for synoptic scale (i.e., large scale) atmospheric motions
        that the first three terms on the right-hand side of <xref ref="eqn-omega-w-relationship-full" text="type-global" />
        are 1<ndash />2 orders of magnitude smaller than the fourth term on the right-hand side so that
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-omega-w-relationship-approximate" number="yes">
            \omega \approx -w \rho g
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        The negative sign in <xref ref="eqn-omega-w-relationship-approximate" text="type-global" />
        reflects the opposing relationship <m>\omega</m> has with <m>w</m>.
    </p>

</subsection>